apture-workers/multivariable/14050604/example-3054.json" "file_sha256" "68229a275409fac4a8c441245885181942605d93d5ef27b8ba79c4762449d1ed"} {"question" "e-3056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/example-3056.json" "file_sha256" "f2fd39dbf95ccc4f10eb196d3e9baf5da6cae1bdd9763089b70ba9437a682e1a"} {"question" "e-3056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})p(t)+{e}^{2t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}p(t)+{e}^{2t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}p(0)+{e}^{2(0)}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+1(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =3\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {3}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i -  \\mathbf j).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/example-3056.json" "file_sha256" "f2fd39dbf95ccc4f10eb196d3e9baf5da6cae1bdd9763089b70ba9437a682e1a"}] "usage" {"q-33096" [] "e-3055" [] "e-3054" [] "e-3056" []} "basis" 1753 "retractions" [[17592186120163 :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = e^{-t}p(t)$ at the point where $t = 0$, if $p(0) = i + j + k$ and $p^{′}(0) = i - j$."] [17592186119481 :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sin^3{t}, \\:\\cos^3{t} \\right\\rangle$ at an arbitrary point where $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$."] [17592186119481 :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf i + y(t) \\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin^{3} t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos^{3} t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin^{2} t\\cos t, 3\\cos^{2} t(-\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= 3\\sin t\\cos t⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\begin{vmatrix}3\\sin t\\cos t & \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + (-\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\ = & 3\\sin t\\cos t\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\mid 3\\sin t\\cos t \\mid \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 3\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= (r^{′}(t))/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sin t\\cos t⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩}{3\\sin t\\cos t} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119480 :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+3t\\,\\mathbf j+2t^2\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,3,2)$?"] [17592186119480 :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf j + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j + z(t)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,3,2)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + 3j + 2k \\\\\ni + 3tj + 2t^{2}k &= i + 3j + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above equation, the first component in each of the two vectors is equal to $1$. Equating the other two components gives the system\n\n$$\n{3t = 3; 2t^{2} = 2\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $t=1$. This value satisfies the second equation too. Therefore, the point $P(1,3,2)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{2})k \\\\\n&= 0i + 3j + 4tk \\\\\n&= 3j + 4tk \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= 3j + 4(1)k \\\\\n&= 3j + 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,3,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= (r^{′}(1))/(‖r^{′}(1)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3j + 4k}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}j + \\frac{4}{5}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119482 :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k$."] [17592186119482 :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t}p(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t})p(t) + e^{2t}\\frac{d}{dt}(p(t)) \\\\\n&= 2e^{2t}p(t) + e^{2t}p^{′}(t) \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= 2e^{2(0)}p(0) + e^{2(0)}p^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= 2(i - j - k) + 1(i - j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 3i - 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (0)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= (r^{′}(0))/(‖r^{′}(0)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3i - 3j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i - j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i - j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117499 17592186117500 17592186117501 17592186117502 17592186117503] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117498 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117498 :answer-field/id] [17592186117498 :answer-field/key] [17592186117498 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
